# native_contacts.in - CPPTRAJ
#
# Fraction of native protein-ligand contacts (Q) over the production run.
#
# Usage:
#   cpptraj -i native_contacts.in
# Run inside each replicate directory.
#
# Replace before running:
#   SYSTEM -> system name          (e.g. BRSV_L_isoquercetin)
#   LIG    -> ligand resname       (e.g. TIL)
#   NRES   -> last protein residue (e.g. 2161)
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Conventions, kept identical across all systems and replicates:
#
#   DRY topology and trajectory (solvent and counter-ions stripped).
#
#   Reference = frame 1 of the trajectory being analysed. Same convention as
#   RMSD and RMSF, so the three analyses can be read together: a drop in Q and
#   a rise in RMSD then describe departure from the same structure. Because
#   each replicate has its own N(0), Q is reported per replicate and never
#   averaged across replicates.
#
#   Heavy atoms only (!@H=). Including hydrogens multiplies each real contact
#   by however many H atoms the group carries, making the absolute count depend
#   on group composition and not comparable with published values.
#
#   4.5 A cutoff.
#
#   Q = N(t)/N(0). N(0) is the number of pairs listed in the native section of
#   contacts_list.dat, above the "# Non-native Contacts" line.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Sanity check after running: the non-native column at frame 1 of
# native_contacts.dat must be 0. If it is not, the reference is not frame 1 of
# this trajectory.
#
# Outputs:
#   native_contacts.dat  frame | native | non-native
#   contacts_list.dat    atom pairs, with occupancy fraction
#   contacts_byres.dat   contacts aggregated by residue pair

parm SYSTEM_solvated_dry.prmtop

reference SYSTEM_solvated_prod_dry.nc 1 [ref]

trajin SYSTEM_solvated_prod_dry.nc

nativecontacts :1-NRES&!@H= :LIG&!@H= \
  ref [ref] \
  distance 4.5 \
  savenonnative \
  out native_contacts.dat \
  writecontacts contacts_list.dat \
  resout contacts_byres.dat

go
quit
